Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero window session tests

Run from the source tree with its existing own compiler:
  bash review-tests/wm-sessions/run.sh

The shell/awk harness substitutes the current wm/init.zero.part and
wm/switch.zero.part into three original Zero scenario templates. It does not
test an old copied implementation. All session operations and assertions
execute as native programs compiled by build/zero --host.

sessions.zero.in has50 numbered assertion sites and exercises:
- UEFI-sized allocation:88 slots, two initially open, first-prompt state.
- Independent unfinished input, overflow and selected volume; global LF state.
- First-focus prompt versus silent later focus.
- New/close/reuse and zeroed reused input/output metadata.
- Explicit scrolling layout and toggle back to tiling.
- Invalid numeric focus without losing input or changing focus.
- Canary preservation below all slots and above the owned session region.
- Creating up to all88 slots, then refusing the next allocation.
- Closing back to one terminal and refusing to close the last terminal.

init-bounds.zero.in has eight initialization scenarios:3MiB capacity344,
minimum one-slot pool, one byte too small, unaligned base, address wrap,
IA32 address ceiling, zero base and missing handoff.

crlf.zero.in has20 assertions. It prepares unfinished input in window2,
executes a deferred focus with pending CRLF suppression, checks that the
framing flag survives, then applies the terminal LF framing rule and verifies
that the unfinished command was not submitted. It also covers a pending LF
across new-window creation and confirms the original input survives. Actual
UART transport and the integrated parser are covered by the VM regression.

Artifacts are written only to build/wm-sessions. Logs and source/compiler
hashes are written to verification/wm-sessions-0.8.0. This does not start a VM,
touch a disk fixture or verify graphical/keyboard/command-engine integration.
Those checks belong to the integrated VM suite.
